 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **MobileNeRF**: Optimized for mobile inference by removing heavy MLPs ($A$ and $F$) from the inference path, replacing them with texture lookups [S1]. Training is slower due to larger MLPs (384 hidden units) [S1].
-*   **Pyramid NeRF**: Enhances training speed by 15x and inference speed by 805x relative to the original NeRF [S3]. It improves structural and perceptual resemblance [S3].
-*   **Residual-NeRF**: Improves depth perception in static scenes with transparent objects and speeds up training by leveraging a pre-trained background model [S4]. The ablation variant (Residual-NeRF-S) performs worse in depth and view reconstruction for partially opaque objects [S4].
-*   **MIMO-DONeRF**: Improves inference speed compared to standard DONeRF by processing samples in parallel [S1].
-*   **DM-NeRF**: Relies on high-quality point clouds; using MVSNet points may degrade quality, hence the use of ground truth points during training [S2, unofficial].
+*   **MobileNeRF**: Optimized for mobile hardware constraints (texture size limits, power of 2 dimensions). Training time is increased due to larger MLPs (384 hidden units), but inference is accelerated by leveraging rasterization pipelines [S1].
+*   **Pyramid NeRF**: Offers significant speedups: 15x faster training and 805x faster inference compared to original NeRF [S3].
+*   **Residual-NeRF-S**: Simpler architecture than Residual-NeRF with Mixnet, but results in inferior depth and view reconstruction quality [S4].
+*   **DM-NeRF**: Requires high-quality input data (ground truth dense point clouds) for training to ensure quality, which may be difficult to obtain for real-world scenes [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF architectures have evolved from the single-MLP baseline [S1] to address limitations in training speed, inference latency, memory usage, and specific scene types (dynamic, transparent, manipulable). Key developments include texture-based approaches for mobile devices [S1], frequency-optimized pyramid structures [S3], residual modeling for transparency [S4], and point-based decompositions for manipulation [S2, unofficial].
+NeRF architectures have evolved from the original two-MLP design [Parent](11313-model-architecture-modifications.md) to address specific limitations in speed, memory, and functionality. MobileNeRF [S1] was introduced to enable neural field rendering on mobile devices by exploiting rasterization. Pyramid NeRF [S3] was proposed to accelerate training and inference through frequency optimization and network segmentation. Residual-NeRF [S4] was developed to improve depth perception and training speed for scenes with transparent objects. DM-NeRF [S2, unofficial] extends Point-NeRF for scene decomposition and manipulation.
 
